Tracing the actual code: convertToAssets takes the escrow branch only when t_CurrentAccount != address(0) and
  _isWithdrawRequestPending(t_CurrentAccount) is true, i.e. only for an account whose own yield tokens have already been
  handed to the withdraw request manager and are no longer valued by the PT/SY share price. That branch is the correct
  valuation for that state, not a hijack: once _initiateWithdraw runs, ERC20(yieldToken).approve(...) +
  withdrawRequestManager.initiateWithdraw(...) transfers the yield tokens out into the cooldown escrow, so
  super.convertToAssets (which prices the vault's held yield token) would be the stale/wrong number, and
  getWithdrawRequestValue(address(this), account, asset, shares) is the per-account, per-share pro-rata claim on the
  escrowed sUSDe/USDe. The branch is also strictly account-scoped by the transient t_CurrentAccount, so it cannot leak
  into any other account's valuation.

  The "steer a third party onto the cheaper branch" half of the claim has no path in this file: _initiateWithdraw is
  internal and receives account from the caller frame in AbstractYieldStrategy, which binds it to the position owner;
  nothing here lets an attacker create a request against someone else's account. _postLiquidation does not create a
  request either — it calls tokenizeWithdrawRequest(liquidateAccount, liquidator, sharesToLiquidator), which by its own
  comment "if there is no withdraw request then this will be a noop," and when there is one it merely splits an
  already-existing request pro rata to the shares actually seized during a liquidation that had to be independently
  justified by the account being undercollateralized. Liquidation eligibility is evaluated with the same convertToAssets
  valuation the account itself has, so no branch switch is induced by the liquidator.

  The "inflated escrow valuation" half rests entirely on getWithdrawRequestValue, whose implementation is in
  IWithdrawRequestManager and is not present in the supplied code. Nothing in this file shows it returning anything
  other than the escrowed claim's fair value denominated in asset, and for an expired PT the PT→SY redemption is 1:1, so
  the escrow claim and the PT price coincide rather than diverging. No rounding direction, no unit mismatch, and no
  double-count is demonstrable from the code shown — the shares argument is passed straight through so the value is
  pro-rata, and hasRequest == false falls through to super.convertToAssets, a safe default.

  There is no guard missing here; there is an intended state-dependent valuation, an account-scoped transient guard that
  confines it, and an unproven premise (escrow value > fair value) that would have to be established in code that is
  not shown. Under the tie-break this cannot survive.

  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/staking/AbstractStakingStrategy.sol:convertToAssets:51|class=C21|The escrow branch is gated by the
  account-scoped t_CurrentAccount transient and only triggers after that same account's yield tokens have actually been
  transferred into the withdraw request manager by _initiateWithdraw, making getWithdrawRequestValue the correct
  pro-rata valuation rather than an inflated one; no path lets an attacker open a request against a third party
  (_postLiquidation's tokenizeWithdrawRequest is a no-op absent a pre-existing request), and the claimed inflation
  depends on unshown manager code.
